from .data import ROOT

# Answers are specific to the actual implementation. No fabricated empirical claims.
TOPICS = [
('What credit-risk problem did you solve?', 'Retrospective prioritization of existing cardholders for review using a next-month payment-event label.', 'Would this score new loan applicants?', 'New applicants may lack these repayment histories; selection bias and domain shift require new data, outcome definitions and independent validation.'),
('Can your probability actually be called PD?', 'It estimates the UCI next-month default-payment event, with held-out calibration checks; it is not bank-grade contractual PD.', 'What definition is missing?', 'The precise contractual default trigger, delinquency cure rules and regulatory horizon are not available. A score cannot be relabelled as lifetime or regulatory default probability.'),
('What is LGD?', 'Loss given default is the fraction of exposure lost conditional on default, after recovery and relevant costs.', 'Did you estimate it?', 'No recovery observations exist. LGD is a labelled scenario input rather than a trained target. Real estimation needs defaulted accounts, recovery timing and discounting.'),
('What is EAD?', 'Exposure at default is the amount outstanding at the moment of default.', 'Is the credit limit EAD?', 'No. Limits are observed, actual default-time drawdowns are not. Our scenario uses assumed exposure separately from granted limits.'),
('What does expected loss mean?', 'EL multiplies event/default probability, conditional severity and default-time exposure; our result is a scenario proxy.', 'Why is it a proxy here?', 'Using a payment-event score in place of contractual PD and assumed LGD/EAD cannot produce a valid provision or actual bank loss estimate.'),
('Why choose UCI rather than Home Credit?', 'The official small public download is reproducible without competition credentials and includes repayment behaviour.', 'What richness did you give up?', 'Income, employment, prior applications and richer borrower context are absent. The project makes fewer financial claims rather than inventing missing quantities.'),
('Why use logistic regression?', 'It is a regularized interpretable benchmark with a log-odds link and efficient training.', 'Is it fully interpretable?', 'Coefficients depend on encoding, scaling, correlated features and specification. Penalized predictive coefficients are not automatically inferential p-values.'),
('Why use XGBoost?', 'Shallow boosted trees capture nonlinear behavioural relationships and interactions.', 'How did you control complexity?', 'Fixed shallow depth, learning rate, subsampling and regularization appear in src/modeling.py. Validation chooses among model/calibration pairs; no test-driven tuning occurs.'),
('Why include a base-rate model?', 'It makes discrimination and probability gains relative to a no-information score visible.', 'What is its Brier score?', 'For prevalence pi, a correctly matched constant predictor has expected Brier pi times one minus pi. The pipeline measures actual holdout Brier rather than substituting that expectation.'),
('Why is accuracy misleading here?', 'A majority-class rule can look accurate while missing every positive event.', 'What did you emphasize?', 'Average precision, ROC-AUC, KS, calibration and review trade-offs. Precision depends on prevalence; reported comparison classification metrics use a conventional threshold separately from the review policy.'),
('What is ROC-AUC?', 'The probability a random positive receives a higher score than a random negative, with ties handled.', 'What does it ignore?', 'It does not encode probability calibration, business costs or review capacity; strong global discrimination may still perform poorly in a critical operating region.'),
('What is average precision?', 'A recall-increment weighted average of precision across score thresholds.', 'Is it identical to PR-AUC?', 'No. Trapezoidal integration in precision–recall space differs. We report average precision explicitly, and its reference level is positive prevalence.'),
('What is KS?', 'The maximum separation between positive and negative score cumulative distributions.', 'Is it a policy threshold?', 'Our KS statistic summarizes discrimination, not an automatic optimal operating point. Review thresholds are validation-score quantiles under a capacity scenario.'),
('What is the Brier score?', 'The mean squared difference between predicted probability and binary event.', 'Why select by it?', 'It is a proper probability score combining calibration and discrimination/resolution. Because the decision layer uses probabilities, the rule predeclares lowest validation Brier, then log loss.'),
('What does calibration mean?', 'Among clients with similar estimated event probability, the observed event frequency should be similar.', 'Does a calibrated client have a guaranteed outcome?', 'No. Calibration is a population frequency property dependent on event definition, horizon and distribution; individual outcomes remain uncertain.'),
('How did sigmoid calibration work?', 'A logistic mapping was learned on raw probability log-odds in a dedicated calibration partition.', 'Why not fit on test?', 'Fitting the mapping on test would make evaluation optimistic. Calibration and selection use separate partitions and the mapping is frozen before test scoring.'),
('Why compare isotonic calibration?', 'A monotone nonparametric map can capture nonlinear probability distortion.', 'What are its risks?', 'Small calibration sets cause stepwise overfitting and score ties. Ranking can change through ties; selection uses independent validation Brier rather than assuming isotonic is best.'),
('AUC improved but calibration worsened. Which would you choose?', 'For this probability-based decision simulation, follow the predeclared validation Brier rule and inspect ranking and operating trade-offs.', 'Would every bank choose that?', 'No. A review-only ranking application might emphasize precision at capacity; pricing and loss estimation require reliable probabilities. The objective must be specified before test evaluation.'),
('Why use separate partitions?', 'Training learns preprocessing/model parameters, calibration learns maps, validation selects candidates and policy, and test estimates final generalization.', 'Why not refit after selection?', 'This portfolio preserves a frozen, auditable fitted bundle. Refitting would require a new calibration design and fresh evaluation rather than reusing the reported test metrics.'),
('How did you prevent duplicate contamination?', 'All identical model-input profiles are grouped into the same fold, excluding identifier, demographics and target from the grouping hash.', 'Why exclude demographics from grouping?', 'Two clients with the same scoring inputs but different audit-only demographics must still share one partition. Identifier disjointness alone does not prevent repeated scoring profiles across partitions.'),
('What leakage risks remained?', 'Target leakage is blocked by an explicit predictor allowlist and preprocessing is train-fitted.', 'Can historical payment features leak?', 'They are legitimate only if available at the snapshot before the next-month label. Precise bank timestamps and event-triggered updates would need independent availability checks in production.'),
('Why not a time split?', 'All clients share the documented historical feature window and no distinct origination/cohort timestamps exist.', 'What can random holdout demonstrate?', 'Only approximate same-sample generalization. It cannot establish stability across future macroeconomic regimes or lending-policy changes.'),
('How did you engineer utilization?', 'Latest and average historical statement amounts divided by granted limit.', 'Why keep negative or above-one ratios?', 'Negative bills may reflect account credits and limits can be exceeded. They are audited rather than automatically deleted; utilization is not a measure of income or affordability.'),
('What is payment-to-positive-bill?', 'Mean historical payment divided by mean clipped-positive bills with a protective denominator floor.', 'Does it measure repayment coverage?', 'Only a behavioural proxy. Monthly alignment and contractual due amounts are insufficient to interpret it as exact instalment coverage; large ratios can arise with very small bills.'),
('How did you treat repayment status codes?', 'Train-fitted one-hot encoding preserves distinct raw categories; delay aggregates use positive codes only.', 'What about undocumented codes?', 'We do not assign unsupported meanings to nonpositive categories. Unknown inference categories are ignored by the encoder, and schema/availability monitoring is necessary.'),
('What is your hypothesis-testing strategy?', 'Training-only prespecified feature comparisons with effect sizes and Holm adjustment across the declared family.', 'What is the null?', 'Mann–Whitney uses identical outcome-group distributions; chi-square uses feature/outcome independence. These are association tests, not causal or separately confirmed experiments.'),
('Why Mann–Whitney rather than only t-tests?', 'The chosen financial variables can be skewed and have extreme values, making rank-based comparisons useful.', 'Does it always test medians?', 'No. It tests distributional differences; a location/median-shift interpretation requires comparable shapes. Ties and dependence affect inference.'),
('Statistical versus practical significance?', 'A small p-value need not imply a useful review signal; effect size and held-out operating results matter.', 'Which effect sizes did you use?', 'Rank-biserial for continuous/rank comparisons, Cramer’s V for categorical association, and point-biserial correlations. Sample size can make weak effects statistically detectable.'),
('What is a Wilson interval?', 'A binomial proportion interval with better boundary behavior than a naive Wald interval.', 'What does it assume?', 'Approximately independent Bernoulli observations with a common proportion within the segment; grouping identical profiles reduces contamination but does not establish real client independence.'),
('Why calculate VIF?', 'It checks redundancy in the reduced inferential design before interpreting coefficients.', 'Why not the entire feature matrix?', 'Engineered summaries plus raw components can be strongly redundant. We report VIF on a deliberately reduced model, not falsely claim the whole prediction design lacks multicollinearity.'),
('What did the inferential logistic model estimate?', 'Conditional log-odds associations for standardized limit, utilization, delay count and payment intensity.', 'What do robust standard errors fix?', 'HC1 changes covariance estimates under some variance misspecification; it does not correct omitted-variable bias, dependence, selection bias or an incorrect logit functional form.'),
('How did you choose thresholds?', 'Validation-score quantiles define relative-risk bands; upper-quartile review is an explicit capacity assumption.', 'Are they optimal?', 'No cost or profitability objective is observed. Ties and population drift alter review fractions; a bank must choose constraints and economics before setting policy.'),
('Why not reject everyone above a PD threshold?', 'Scores omit affordability, customer economics, recoverability, uncertainty and legal/governance constraints; review is more defensible than automatic rejection.', 'What would economic optimization require?', 'Incremental lending value, funding/operating costs, expected loss, exposure policy, customer behaviour and constraints, evaluated on representative outcomes with counterfactual selection issues addressed.'),
('What is SHAP?', 'A Shapley-value attribution assigning feature contributions relative to a defined model baseline.', 'What implementation did you use?', 'Exact XGBoost TreeSHAP via pred_contribs, plotted with the SHAP package; contributions are checked to sum to raw tree margins, not calibrated probabilities.'),
('Why can SHAP not establish causality?', 'It explains the fitted prediction function under attribution assumptions, not the effect of intervening on a borrower feature.', 'How do correlated features matter?', 'Attribution allocation depends on dependence assumptions and encoded representations. Redundant repayment features can split importance; causal inference requires a separate identification design.'),
('What are the main risk drivers?', 'The executed shap_global.csv ranks mean absolute raw-tree log-odds contributions on a reproducible held-out sample.', 'Can you quote them safely?', 'Use the generated ranking, not a generic claim about income. Check the selected model: tree benchmark explanations are explicitly separate if another model wins.'),
('How did you analyze fairness?', 'Held-out sex and age groups include counts, event rates, AUC, Brier, average calibration gaps, error rates and simulated selection fractions.', 'Is the model fair?', 'No such claim follows. Base-rate differences, uncertainty, proxy effects and competing fairness definitions require context and governance; excluding demographics is insufficient.'),
('What is PSI?', 'A binned population divergence summing proportion differences times log proportion ratios.', 'Did you observe production drift?', 'No. Random partition comparison and a labelled synthetic limit shift demonstrate the framework only. Reference-bin choices, smoothing and sample size affect the result.'),
('What happens if the applicant population changes?', 'Reassess input availability, feature/score distributions, delayed outcome rates and calibration; escalate to human model governance.', 'Should PSI trigger immediate retraining?', 'No. Identify data issues and changes in policy/population, evaluate with mature labels and independent validation; retraining and recalibration need controlled release decisions.'),
('How was SQL genuine?', 'Each query ran against the generated DuckDB portfolio table and exported its results.', 'What analyses exist?', 'Split-level portfolio summary, held-out band segmentation, subgroup monitoring, score deciles and assumed high-risk exposure loss; observed limits are explicitly distinct from scenario EAD.'),
('What assumptions did you make?', 'Common source snapshot, same-sample holdout applicability, capacity-based review quantiles and explicitly assumed LGD/EAD.', 'Which are most fragile?', 'Geographic/time transfer, unknown contractual default definition and unobserved loss severity/exposure prevent production underwriting and loss estimation claims.'),
('What would differ inside a real bank?', 'Bank-specific data governance, as-of feature availability, contractual targets, temporal validation, affordability and economic policy.', 'What operational controls are needed?', 'Access control, audit logs, model inventory/versioning, independent validation, monitoring ownership, manual override and controlled deployment; this local portfolio does not implement bank infrastructure.'),
('How did you test the dashboard?', 'Artifact-only Streamlit pages are exercised with AppTest, and the final audit launches a temporary server and checks its health.', 'Does that establish usability?', 'No. Programmatic rendering and server health detect runtime failures; they do not replace accessibility, browser interaction, security or bank user-acceptance testing.'),
('What can you claim on your resume?', 'Executed dataset size, held-out metrics and explicitly retrospective review-simulation results from saved artifacts.', 'What must you avoid?', 'No real bank profit uplift, deployment, bank-grade PD, causal drivers, true production drift or fairness certification. The generated resume points directly to numerical sources.'),
]

def write_guide(summary):
    intro=f"""# Interview guide: defend the actual project

## Your opening explanation

I built a historical existing-cardholder decision-support project using {summary['rows']:,} UCI records. I compared a base-rate predictor, logistic regression and XGBoost, then independently fitted probability calibration. The predeclared validation rule selected {summary['selected']}. Held-out AUC is {summary['test_metrics']['roc_auc']:.4f}; Brier is {summary['test_metrics']['brier']:.4f}. My contribution is the separation of statistical evidence, model scores, review-capacity assumptions and hypothetical losses rather than a claim of production loan approval.

## Beginner to advanced foundations

Credit risk is uncertainty about meeting contractual payment obligations. A model predicts a defined outcome over a defined horizon. Bank PD is default probability for that contract/horizon; LGD is conditional loss severity; EAD is default-time exposure. Their product forms an elementary expected-loss framework. This project uses an event probability and assumed severity/exposure, so its loss is a scenario proxy.

Logistic regression maps a linear predictor through the sigmoid function; log-odds coefficients are conditional associations. XGBoost sequentially adds regularized trees to reduce a differentiable loss, allowing nonlinearities. Class imbalance means most observations may be non-events; accuracy can hide poor event capture. ROC-AUC evaluates ranking; average precision weights precision by recall increments. KS measures separation of conditional score CDFs. Brier evaluates squared probability errors. Reliability curves compare average scores with event rates; ranking and calibration are different properties.

Leakage occurs when training/selection sees information unavailable at scoring or evaluation. Overfitting fits idiosyncrasies rather than stable patterns. Separate grouped holdouts, fixed model settings, train-only preprocessing and independent calibration/selection reduce these risks. A threshold converts scores into an action under stated capacity/cost constraints; it is not determined by AUC. SHAP attributes the model function relative to a baseline, not causality. PSI summarizes binned distribution change without proving deterioration. Fairness requires context, subgroup uncertainty and governance, not one similar metric. Statistical significance concerns evidence against a null under assumptions; practical significance concerns decision usefulness.

Mathematically, logistic p=1/(1+exp(-xβ)); Brier=mean((p-y)^2); KS=sup_s |F_positive(s)-F_negative(s)|; PSI=sum_i (q_i-r_i) log(q_i/r_i). Inferential odds ratios are exp(β), conditional on the chosen design. Calibration estimates E[Y|score] under the sampled distribution. A proper scoring rule encourages truthful probabilities in expectation, while rank metrics are invariant to strictly monotonic transformations. Isotonic steps can introduce ties. No mathematical technique overcomes mismatched targets or unavailable bank evidence.

## Questions and deeper follow-ups
"""
    blocks=[]
    for i,(q,a,f,d) in enumerate(TOPICS,1):
        blocks.append(f'### Q{i}. {q}\n\n**Strong answer:** {a}\n\n**Possible follow-up:** {f}\n\n**Deeper answer:** {d}\n')
    (ROOT/'reports/interview_guide.md').write_text(intro+'\n'.join(blocks),encoding='utf-8')
